{"ts": 1789777669.962306, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Let me reason carefully about the shrinkage rather than just quoting the in-sample 0.824.\n\n**Selection bias.** I evaluated ~50 specifications and am submitting the maximum. With n=5000, the SE of an annualized Sharpe is \u2248 \u221a(252/5000) \u2248 0.22. The max of ~50 correlated draws sits roughly 1.5\u20132 SE above the truth from selection alone, implying shrinkage of ~0.2\u20130.35.\n\n**Evidence the signal is partly real.** Three things argue against pure noise:\n- The components were individually meaningful *before* combining \u2014 f33 at 0.550 is ~2.5 SE on its own, and f25 and f5 were both top-tier singles.\n- The sign structure is stable and economically coherent: every flip degrades sharply (0.824 \u2192 0.32/0.31/0.18), which is not how noise behaves.\n- The gain from 1\u21922\u21923 features (0.550 \u2192 0.699 \u2192 0.824) tracks the \u221ak diversification path almost exactly. That's the fingerprint of averaging several genuinely weak-but-real signals, not of overfitting one lucky interaction.\n\n**Evidence for caution.** The whole top of the cross-section is tightly bunched (dozens of triples in 0.68\u20130.82), which is what you'd see if I'm largely reshuffling the same modest common signal and harvesting noise at the margin. The bar of 1.023 is the environment's own estimate that specs of this class don't hold up at face value.\n\nNetting these: I shrink 0.824 toward roughly 0.45\u20130.50. I'll set my mean at **0.47** and use a wide sd of **0.32** \u2014 wide because it must cover genuine regime change and the real possibility that the diversification gain was partly illusory, leaving meaningful mass below zero."}
